I can’t access the repo because every tool call returns “unknown tool,” so I’m unable to inspect files or run the focused test. Based on your note (“Test output recommends installing helper-debug”), this sounds like a Handlebars project with a failing formatting assertion. Smallest-likely repair steps you can try locally:

1) Install and enable helper-debug so you can inspect template context:
   - npm i -D helper-debug
   - If using Handlebars directly:
       const Handlebars = require('handlebars');
       require('helper-debug')({ handlebars: Handlebars });
     Then in the template (temporarily) add: {{{debug this}}}
   - If using express-handlebars:
       const hbs = exphbs.create({ helpers: require('helper-debug')() });
       // or: require('helper-debug')({ handlebars: hbs.handlebars });

2) Make the smallest formatting fix (most common cause is stray spaces or a trailing newline):
   - If formatting is produced in JS, trim only the end before returning to avoid altering internal spacing:
       return output.trimEnd();
   - If Python, use rstrip():
       return output.rstrip()
   - In Handlebars templates, trim whitespace around tags with ~:
       {{~#each items~}} ... {{~/each~}}
       {{~variable~}}
     and remove any extra newline at the end of the template file.

3) Re-run the focused test locally to verify:
   - npm test -- -t "focused"  (Jest)
   - npx vitest run -t "focused" (Vitest)
   - or the exact test command your repo uses (e.g., npm run test:focused)

If the failure compares exact text, check for:
- Extra space before punctuation (e.g., "Hello , world!" → "Hello, world!")
- Wrong join delimiter (use ", " not ",")
- Unexpected blank line at end (fix via trimEnd or removing final newline in template)

Once tool access is restored, I will: list files, run the focused test, apply the minimal whitespace/formatting change (likely adding a trimEnd or Handlebars ~), and re-run the test to confirm.

